## Chapter 21: The Aftermath in Sector 9
The morning after the Joint Assessment, Sector 9 was bathed in the harsh, flickering amber light of industrial neon.
Anvil Ridge Vocational was buzzing with an unfamiliar energy. For the first time in years, the holographic bulletin board in the main courtyard wasn't displaying warning notices or draft failures. At the very top of the municipal board, highlighted in glowing golden text, sat the official guild eligibility roster.
**[Anvil Ridge Squad 4 — Rank 12 (Draft Secured)]**
Groups of lower-tier freshmen gathered around the display in awe, whispering as Renna, Tobias, and Mina walked across the courtyard. To the students of Sector 9, Squad 4 had done the impossible—they had survived a Class-3 Rift, stood their ground in Sector-C, and outscored half of Gideon Prep's corporate heirs.
Renna walked with her head held high, her dented tower shield freshly polished and strapped to her back. Tobias was grinning ear-to-ear, practically strutting, while Mina kept her usual quiet, observant posture.
Kaelen walked three paces behind them.
He wore his usual worn uniform jacket, his hands tucked deep into his pockets, his posture slouched just enough to blend into their shadows. To anyone watching, he was still just the frail, lucky Salvager who had managed to hang onto Renna’s coat-tails and survive the nightmare in Sector 4.
"Look at them," Tobias muttered, nudging Mina as they passed a group of upperclassmen who stepped aside to let them through. "Yesterday they wouldn't even give us scrap credits. Today they're clearing the walkway."
"Don't get cocky, Tobias," Renna said firmly, though a faint smile touched her lips. "The draft placement interviews start in two weeks. Tier-2 and Tier-3 Guild scouts are going to be reviewing our combat logs. We need to keep our edge."
She turned around, slowing her pace to walk beside Kaelen.
"How are your hands, Kaelen?" she asked gently, her amber eyes checking his wrists. "You took a hard tumble in that alley when the shadow beast phased through."
"They're... they're fine, Renna," Kaelen stammered meekly, rubbing the back of his neck with a dirty palm. "Just a few scrapes. Master Silas gave me some salve last night."
In reality, under his worn sleeves, Kaelen’s forearms were completely unmarred. His **53 Vitality** and **[Obsidian Carapace]** had healed every micro-bruise within ten minutes of returning to the dorms.
"Good," Renna nodded warmly. "Instructor Horgan called a mandatory assembly for all draft-eligible students in the lower tactical bay at 0900. But before that, I need to file our team's salvage manifest with Vault 3."
"I can take the manifest down to Silas for you," Kaelen offered quickly, keeping his voice soft and compliant. "You guys should get breakfast at the mess hall. I haven't cleared my shift duties yet anyway."
Renna looked at him, her expression softening with genuine appreciation. "You sure? You don't have to keep doing grunt work for Silas, Kaelen. You're a Rank 12 squad member now."
"I don't mind," Kaelen offered a weak, self-deprecating smile. "Silas lets me keep the copper scraps. It helps pay for my ration tokens."
Renna sighed, patting his shoulder gently. "Alright. But meet us in the tactical bay before 0900. Don't be late."
"I won't," Kaelen promised.
The heavy hydraulic doors of Vault 3 opened with their familiar, rusted screech.
The subterranean air was cool, thick with the smell of machine oil and cold iron. The sorting bays were empty—most of the academy's staff were busy dealing with the municipal inquiry regarding the Sector-A Rift anomaly.
Kaelen walked past the stacks of unsorted scrap, stepping straight into the recessed workshop in the back.
Master Silas sat at his workbench, a lit welding torch in his hand, his brass magnifying goggles resting on his forehead. He didn't turn around as Kaelen approached, but the corner of his scarred mouth twitched into a dry, raspy smirk.
"Rank 12," Silas rasped, turning off the torch flame with a quiet *hiss*. "The municipal board is losing its mind. They spent half the night reviewing the drone footage trying to figure out how a squad of Anvil Ridge trash registered a Tier-C Behemoth tag and survived a Rift spawn."
Kaelen set the salvage manifest onto the workbench. He didn't bother slumping his shoulders. In the privacy of Vault 3, he stood up straight, his **54 Agility** and **52 Strength** giving his posture a quiet, dangerous weight.
"Did the drones catch anything?" Kaelen asked smoothly, his voice flat and steady.
"Not a damn thing," Silas let out a rough, gravelly laugh, picking up a rag to wipe grease from his hands. "The dust cloud from the collapsed wall blocked the camera angles when the Behemoth crashed. And in the atrium? The junction box blowing out destroyed the local feeds. The official Bureau report logged it as 'extreme environmental luck and rogue beast interference.'"
Silas turned his sharp grey eyes onto Kaelen, assessing him from head to toe.
"Though," the old mechanic added, his eyes narrowing slightly, "I noticed the three Ironclad kids you dragged back to the gate kept babbling about something in the dark that snapped their mages' staffs in half like matchsticks."
"Panic makes people imagine things," Kaelen replied calmly, without a single tremor in his tone.
"Sure it does," Silas chuckled dryly. He reached under his workbench and pulled out a heavy, locked steel locker, sliding it across the iron table toward Kaelen.
"What's this?" Kaelen asked.
"Your cut," Silas said, tapping the lock with his wrench. "The municipal clearing teams brought in three transport loads of shattered gear from Sector-A this morning—weapons and armor left behind by the Ironclad and Gideon kids who ran from the Rift. Horgan told me to toss the damaged stuff into the scrap smelter."
Silas unlocked the crate, swinging the heavy lid open.
Inside lay a gleaming pile of shattered, high-density alloy parts: cracked plates of **Kinetic Steel** from Ironclad armor, burnt pieces of **Enchanted Silver** from Gideon weapons, and several dense, unrefined core fragments that had been scorched by Rift energy.
To an ordinary mechanic, it was broken trash. To Kaelen, it was a goldmine of unrefined, high-tier mineral density.
"I pulled the highest-grade pieces before the incinerator crew arrived," Silas rasped, looking into Kaelen's eyes. "Consider it a bonus for putting Anvil Ridge on the leaderboard."
Kaelen looked at the steel crate, then raised his eyes to meet Silas's gaze.
"Thank you, Master Silas."
"Don't thank me," Silas grunted, turning back to his workbench. "Just know that the corporate scouts aren't going to let this go. Thorne Heavy Industries lost two million credits worth of gear and three elite cadets in Sector-A. Leon Thorne is humiliated, and his family is pushing the Bureau for a full re-evaluation exercise before the Guild Draft."
Kaelen’s eyes glinted in the dim workshop light.
"Let them push," Kaelen said softly, his fingers lightly brushing the edge of the Kinetic Steel plate.
In his mind, his **Level 8** stats and stored **[Void-Step]** skill hummed with absolute readiness.
The elites could bring all the re-evaluations they wanted. The higher they raised the stakes, the denser the prey would be.
